<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Oration on the Dignity of Man</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1486">1486</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Oration on the Dignity of Man is a speech written in 1486 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giovanni-pico-della-mirandola">Giovanni Pico della Mirandola</a> as the introduction to his 900 theses, which he planned to defend in public debate in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. It argues that humans, unlike other creatures, have no fixed place in creation and can shape their own nature by free choice. Published after his death, it is often called a manifesto of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> humanism.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Pico della <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mirandola">Mirandola</a> belonged to the family that had long held the Castle of Mirandola, but he left his share of the principality to his two brothers to devote himself to study. In 1477, aged 14, he went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bologna">Bologna</a> to study canon law. From 1479 he spent seven years in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferrara">Ferrara</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/padua">Padua</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, studying Greek, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syriac">Syriac</a> and Arabic. He composed the Oration in 1486, while in his twenties, as the opening speech of a public disputation of his 900 theses planned for early 1487.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The speech sets out a Neoplatonic account of creation. After God had made all creatures, there was no room left in the chain of being, from angels to worms, so he made man without a fixed place. Humans can learn from and imitate any creature: when they philosophize they rise towards the angels and communion with God, and when they neglect their intellect they vegetate. For Pico the root of human dignity was that only human beings could change themselves through their own free will.</p><p>Pico also spoke of the three highest choirs of angels: the Seraphim, who stand for charity, the Cherubim for intelligence and the Thrones for justice. He gave most weight to the Cherubim, whose status is reached through contemplation, and argued that a philosopher is a creature of Heaven and not of earth. The theses themselves combined Platonism, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neoplatonism">Neoplatonism</a>, Aristotelianism, Hermeticism and Kabbalah, and Pico asked that his youth should not discredit them.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The disputation never took place. Pope Innocent VIII suspended it and set up a commission to examine the theses for heresy. He condemned the theses in general but declared their author free from censure, in a document written on 5 August 1487 and issued that December. The Oration itself remained unpublished until 1496.</p><p>Pico's view of human capacity influenced the arts, helping to raise writers and painters from their medieval role as artisans to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> ideal of the artist as creative genius. The Pico Project, a collaboration between the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bologna">Bologna</a> and Brown University, and others have called the speech the Manifesto of the Renaissance.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Oration on the Dignity of Man” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Giovanni Pico della Mirandola’s <b>Oration on the Dignity of Man</b>, written in 1486 to introduce his 900 theses, celebrated the human freedom to rise or fall by choice; Marsilio Ficino sought to reconcile Platonic philosophy with Christian faith; and Erasmus called his own ideal the “philosophy of Christ”,…</blockquote><a href="/movement/renaissance-humanism">Renaissance humanism · Aims</a></li><li><blockquote>The humanist Giovanni Pico della Mirandola, author of the <b>Oration on the Dignity of Man</b>, was a younger son of the family.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-mirandola">Duchy of Mirandola</a></li></ul></section>
